# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
# Feature 23.1 — the Windows shell is a SEPARATE npm project (windows-shell/
# has its own package.json and package-lock.json, so Electron never enters the
# web app's dependency tree). The rule above is root-anchored and would not
# cover its node_modules, and an unignored Electron install is ~300 MB of
# binaries (measured: 309 MB). Its package-lock.json IS tracked, deliberately.
/windows-shell/node_modules
# Feature 23.4 — electron-builder output. Every build writes an installer here
# (~80 MB) plus unpacked application trees. The authoritative installer comes
# from the windows-latest GitHub Actions job and is downloaded as a workflow
# artifact, never committed.
/windows-shell/dist
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# .env.example carries NAMES and placeholders only — never a real credential.
# It is the one env file that must be committed, so every other .env* stays
# ignored and this single negation re-includes it.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Feature 16.2 — Android shell.
# Feature 24.5F — the Windows shell's packaged device runtime, built by
# `npm run windows:runtime` from native-device/. Generated output, exactly like
# android-shell/www below: one source, two package directories, neither tracked.
/windows-shell/runtime/

# android-shell/www is regenerated from the native-device build on every
# `npm run android:sync`, so it is build output, not source. The generator
# (android-shell/*.mjs) and capacitor.config.ts ARE tracked.
/android-shell/www/
# The native android/ project itself is tracked, but it carries its own
# .gitignore (generated by Capacitor) covering build/, .gradle/,
# local.properties, copied web assets, and generated capacitor config.
# local.properties is re-listed here because it contains a machine-specific
# absolute SDK path.
android/local.properties

# Feature 20 — Android release signing material, ignored REPO-WIDE.
#
# android/.gitignore already covers these for the native project. They are
# repeated here without a path prefix so a keystore generated anywhere else —
# the repo root, a scratch folder, a stray `keytool` invocation from the wrong
# working directory — is caught too. A signing key is the one secret in this
# project that cannot be rotated after customers have installed the app, so the
# protection is deliberately broader than the place it is expected to live.
#
# The real keystore is stored OUTSIDE this repository entirely; see
# DEPLOYMENT.md. These rules are a backstop, not the storage plan.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pepk
keystore.properties
